{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity","id":932459,"title":"Wisdom vs Integrity","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-16T03:53:02-07:00","id":932459,"title":"Wisdom vs Integrity","content":"So I need some confirmation about it. Mages are not human, they are observers, detectives and explorers.<br /> <br /> Wisdom degenerates only in response to character action (and inaction in case when he could aid? Im not sure) and when human breaks after seeing brutal murder crime scene a mage is just investigating unaffected. Is that right?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-16T04:28:13-07:00","id":932464,"title":"","content":"If they are unaffected you're most likely doing the scene wrong, mages are still human after all so any reaction that is, is in character of the specific mage. But they won't risk dropping wisdom for seeing that, but depending on the characters some conditions might still be in order.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/523-poseur","name":"Poseur","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"375"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-16T04:56:20-07:00","id":932469,"title":"","content":"Yea, biologically they are human and most likely that body will react. You threw up and got some condition but in the end its not so important to you to haunt you in dreams. <br /> Recklessness is what you should avoid.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Wisdom 8\u201310</b>, High / Enlightened (5 dice) These highest<br /> levels of Wisdom force the mage to walk a careful line. Any minor<br /> Act of Hubris risks degeneration. At this level, any time the<br /> mage uses a spell to accomplish something she could do through<br /> mundane methods with little or no risk poses the chance for<br /> degeneration. When innocent bystanders are affected by your<br /> character\u2019s spells or actions, she is at risk as well.<br /> <b>Wisdom 4\u20137</b>, Medium / Understanding (3 dice): Most<br /> experienced and stable mages fall into this range of Wisdom.<br /> Sometimes, Acts of Hubris happen. But by and large, the mage<br /> acts with basic Wisdom most of the time. Allowing a Sleeper to<br /> witness obvious magic, thus risking greater Paradox, can cause<br /> degeneration. Self-mutilating events such as soul stone creation<br /> risk degeneration. Not attempting to contain a severe Paradox<br /> risks degeneration as well. Forcing a sapient being (whether a<br /> Sleeper, spirit, or anything else) to act counter to its interests,<br /> altering its nature long-term, or binding it to a task all risk<br /> degeneration, as does deliberate and premeditated murder and<br /> violence that leaves its victim with long-term injury.<br /> <b>Wisdom 1\u20133</b>, Low / Falling (1 die): Hubris of this level concerns<br /> all mages. A mage at this precipice could be lost to his<br /> magic at any time. Only the darkest, most selfishly destructive<br /> acts risk degeneration at this point. Killing someone in a fit of<br /> rage, destroying an Awakened soul, allowing a Supernal being<br /> to be consumed by the Fallen World, or dealing with the Abyss<br /> can force the mage through her final loss of Wisdom.<br /> Wisdom 0: A character with no Wisdom is forever lost. His<br /> hubris has overcome him, and he\u2019s become one of \u201cThe Mad.\u201d<br /> His magic leaks into the world, letting the Supernal out wherever<br /> he goes. He cannot control his magic; it controls him. </div> </div> </div>There's an example. Killing is never o good option but premeditated murder, the one that you planed and is 'wisely' done, is a lot better than a crime of passion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-16T06:07:14-07:00","id":932480,"title":"","content":"A question for you guys is &quot;How human are mages in your chronicle?&quot; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-16T06:09:54-07:00","id":932482,"title":"","content":"As they choose to.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-16T07:29:53-07:00","id":932496,"title":"","content":"I keep it with Dave and go with the assumption that mages are monsters.<br /> <br /> On the initial topic: I would still have Resolve + Composure rolls for dealing with horrifying shit, but they obviously will not lose Wisdom over it. Mages also simply do not suffer the long-term psychological damage represented by low Integrity. Sooner or later their natural curiosity and pride wins out over whatever horror they have seen.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-16T07:54:14-07:00","id":932505,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>saibot</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932496#post932496\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I keep it with Dave and go with the assumption that mages are monsters.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this thread reveals the flaw in those kinds of statements. You need to elaborate on what you mean or else people are going to start assuming that the Awakened are all sociopaths or whatever.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-16T08:30:37-07:00","id":932526,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932505#post932505\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You need to elaborate on what you mean or else people are going to start assuming that the Awakened are all sociopaths or whatever.</div> </div> </div> </div>In recent memory, virtually everybody who has held this assumption has refused to accept subsequent clarification or even pre-established context regardless.<br /> <br /> &quot;The fundamental priorities of a mage are more in line with the symbolism-minded supernatural denizens of other worlds in the setting than mundane humans&quot; is easy enough to grasp as the meaning of &quot;mages are monsters&quot; when every other ChroD gameline has you playing monsters of this stripe as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-16T10:18:57-07:00","id":932554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity#post932459\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I need some confirmation about it. Mages are not human, they are observers, detectives and explorers.<br /> <br /> Wisdom degenerates only in response to character action (and inaction in case when he could aid? Im not sure) and when human breaks after seeing brutal murder crime scene a mage is just investigating unaffected. Is that right?</div> </div> </div> </div>More or less. Mages are what happens when you give an ordinary human access to nearly unlimited, nearly unrestricted power. Their power comes from knowledge, and they are irresistably driven to acquire more knowledge. Where Integrity is lost by experiencing trauma, Wisdom is lost by acting carelessly with the power and knowledge a Mage has. An ordinary human might or might not experience a Breaking Point when they witness the scene of a brutal murder, depending on what that human finds traumatic, but a Mage would definitely not experience an Act of Hubris for witnessing the same scene, because it doesn't have anything to do with using their power or knowledge carelessly. Either might experience other effects besides Morality/Integrity loss, at the ST's discretion - trauma isn't what causes Mage's to lose Wisdom, but that doesn't mean they're immune to being traumatized.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932480#post932480\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A question for you guys is &quot;How human are mages in your chronicle?&quot; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I think part of what makes Mage as horrifying as it is, is the fact that the Awakend <i>seem</i> so human. It works because you can easily put yourself into a Mage's shoes. They're not like vampires loosing sight of what it even means to be human, or werewolves driven to hunt and kill and torn between two worlds. They think, feel, and act more or less like humans naturally would if they were suddenly given access to the secrets of the universe and could use that knowledge to re-shape reality. But the scariest part is how that knowledge and that power changes the way you think, feel, and act. It seems like such a natural progression, but when you take a step back and look at how warped and far removed from normal human thoughts, feelings, and actions theirs are, it's quite profound. It just goes to show what a fragile thing the qualities we see as quintessential to humanity really are, and the fact that folks often struggle to see the inhumanity inherent in Mages only drives the message home even more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T09:12:13-07:00","id":932928,"title":"","content":"I think a part of the problem with why people disagree so much about the inhumanity of Mages is a disagreement of what people consider quintessential to humanity, or whether you even feel there <i>is</i> a quintessence or more a sliding gradient of what we consider human and not-human.<br /> <br /> For example, if you talk about how distanced a Mage becomes by their power and knowledge from humanity, one could easily argue that they're not anymore inhuman than they were before; they're simply what a human becomes when they have a set amount of knowledge and power.<br /> <br /> They might be fairly removed from mundane humanity, but does that make them inhuman?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T10:37:34-07:00","id":932956,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">They might be fairly removed from mundane humanity, but does that make them inhuman?</span> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><br /> <br /> You're answering your own question there.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T10:53:17-07:00","id":932965,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932928#post932928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think a part of the problem with why people disagree so much about the inhumanity of Mages is a disagreement of what people consider quintessential to humanity, or whether you even feel there <i>is</i> a quintessence or more a sliding gradient of what we consider human and not-human.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932928#post932928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For example, if you talk about how distanced a Mage becomes by their power and knowledge from humanity, one could easily argue that they're not anymore inhuman than they were before; they're simply what a human becomes when they have a set amount of knowledge and power.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. Is that not a deeply horrifying thought? That the only thing separating us from the warped worldview of a Mage is opportunity?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932928#post932928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They might be fairly removed from mundane humanity, but does that make them inhuman?</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe not. I would certainly say it makes them monsters. And that's the scary part, if these monsters are so human, it kind of implies that humans are pretty darn monstrous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T11:34:31-07:00","id":932981,"title":"","content":"I mean, all you need to do in order to see that is read a history book. Humans do awful things all the time (and good things too!), and Mages are no different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T11:59:23-07:00","id":932988,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932981#post932981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, all you need to do in order to see that is read a history book. Humans do awful things all the time (and good things too!), and Mages are no different.</div> </div> </div> </div>In that way, no, Mages are no different.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T13:36:30-07:00","id":933022,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932981#post932981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, all you need to do in order to see that is read a history book. Humans do awful things all the time (and good things too!), and Mages are no different.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Rules state otherwise. With will alone they pierce and change the very fabric of reality. Those are not tricks, it all comes from understanding. Humans are just children to the Wise, they don't see. They Sleep. <br /> Secrets are what makes mages go onward, they are Obssessed. If they do it recklessly they can go Mad.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T14:46:29-07:00","id":933072,"title":"","content":"That has nothing to do with whether or not Mages do good or bad things, which they do, regardless of whether or not it's condoned by Wisdom.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T14:58:22-07:00","id":933079,"title":"","content":"Also I'd argue that humans are as capable of obsession as Mages are, it's just that Mages as a group are all defined by obsession (because that they are obsessively seeking the truth is why they awaken in the first place!) and therefore they are more obsessive as a type. But I don't think they are superhumanly obsessive, I think they are as obsessive as obsessive humans whose senses are unable to ignore the source of their obsession would be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T15:23:41-07:00","id":933089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933079#post933079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also I'd argue that humans are as capable of obsession as Mages are, it's just that Mages as a group are all defined by obsession (because that they are obsessively seeking the truth is why they awaken in the first place!) and therefore they are more obsessive as a type. But I don't think they are superhumanly obsessive, I think they are as obsessive as obsessive humans whose senses are unable to ignore the source of their obsession would be.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. That's what's so scary.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T21:02:45-07:00","id":933237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932965#post932965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exactly. Is that not a deeply horrifying thought? That the only thing separating us from the warped worldview of a Mage is opportunity?</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest... Not really. Not for me, at least. If anything, it makes them more comprehensible. Even rational. It means that what they're doing isn't based purely on moon logic (maybe not the best euphemism since it may be based on literal moon logic).<br /> <br /> The horrifying part of not understanding a Mage, to me, is the feeling of how ignorant <i>I</i> am, not how alien their modes of operation are to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932965#post932965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe not. I would certainly say it makes them monsters. And that's the scary part, if these monsters are so human, it kind of implies that humans are pretty darn monstrous.</div> </div> </div> </div>That feels like a misapplication of the term monstrous in this context, though. We're not talking about the same sort of monstrosity we might see in, say, a vampire or werewolf; where the entity is intrinsically inimical to humanity. We're talking about it in a context where a person is driven to monstrous action by some force or circumstance.<br /> <br /> That doesn't make Mages monstrous to me or, at least, if it does, no more than any other human being. That's different to me than, say, a walking corpse that has to inflict pain and weakness just to endure another night of unlife.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T21:22:21-07:00","id":933247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932928#post932928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think a part of the problem with why people disagree so much about the inhumanity of Mages is a disagreement of what people consider quintessential to humanity, or whether you even feel there <i>is</i> a quintessence or more a sliding gradient of what we consider human and not-human.</div> </div> </div> </div>Obsessive inflexibility of driving focus is, in fact, one of the defining qualities of most inhuman monsters in the setting. Most gameline-level PCs get ringside tickets to what happens when you smush that trait up against a more rounded humanlike personality and wait for the former to overwhelm the latter.<br /> <br /> Monstrosity and humanity don't have to be opposites for one to be a notable contrast to the other in bulk majority.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-17T21:41:15-07:00","id":933257,"title":"","content":"That's a tricky comparison to make. Mages only have carrots; all the others have sticks. Nothing forces them to do anything they do; they don't have to be obsessive or inflexible. That's all on the characters themselves. As such, I'd say Mages <i>lack</i> that defining quality of inhumanity.<br /> <br /> Mages aren't people who are <i>put</i> in awful situations; they're people who <i>put themselves</i> in awful situations. That's one of the core features of Mage: That you heap your own damnation upon your head.<br /> <br /> There's nothing intrinsically monstrous to a Mage and that's really a good thing because it takes away their excuses when things go to hell.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T00:17:30-07:00","id":933293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933257#post933257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's a tricky comparison to make. Mages only have carrots; all the others have sticks. Nothing forces them to do anything they do</div> </div> </div> </div>Nothing &quot;forces&quot; a vampire to drink blood and nothing &quot;forces&quot; a werewolf to hunt. Nothing &quot;forces&quot; most of the gameline-having PCs to indulge any of their respective hungers, mechanically speaking.<br /> <br /> I am not talking about mechanics in more than the most tangential sense when I say that mages share the personal qualities that put them in close and regular proximity to the traits that are ineluctable drives to less grounded monsters. The Awakening plants a seed of monstrosity just as surely as the Embrace or the First Change or etc does, and the ability to try and defy that nature is not unique to mages regardless of how little impact it has on their ability to literally function outside its bounds. The dearth of sticks is irrelevant to my point.<br /> <br /> The Long-Term Nimbus is a thing - the Awakened serve as attractor fields for mysteries they are personally oriented to investigate; the things that these mysteries affect include, at the bare minimum, persons and locations that they have some significant investment in, and withdrawing that investment has consequences that isolate a character as surely as Quiescence does.<br /> <br /> The Awakened counterpart to draugrdom reduce to a nexus of self-propelled symbolism built around a monomaniacal pursuit of one of your Obsessions so extreme that it replaces your Virtue and isolates you from humanity to such a degree that you can no longer enter the Temenos and are personally the subject of Quiescence.<br /> <br /> The Seers as a sect have an institutional fault line in their Wisdom because despite their worldview of &quot;magic has a price&quot; being largely accurate to broad trends in setting metaphysics the ones who advance furthest up the Pyramid are the ones that will most often pay the price.<br /> <br /> Part of Wisdom is essentially maintenance on what the Supernal definition of your character is based on prior activity, especially through the lens of their Shadow Name and other symbolism-that-tells-the-world-what-you-are. That that symbolism increasingly runs away with itself with lower Wisdom is the reduction to pure defined-actor that the Supernal self becomes when untempered by care for the consequences on the wider world. The urge to name things and so establish dominion over them is the root from which mages' monstrosity grows.<br /> <br /> There is no quintessential humanity save for that <i>don't have to</i> that you're attributing to mages among monsters. That the Awakened exist at the shallower end of the Monster Pool by default does nothing to remove them from it, especially when they're such enthusiastic swimmers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T00:37:27-07:00","id":933302,"title":"","content":"Vamps have Frenzy and werewolves have Kuruth. Werewolves lose Essence and slowly become increasingly vulnerable to Kuruth as they refuse to hunt.<br /> <br /> The vampire must feed.<br /> <br /> The wolf must hunt.<br /> <br /> Mages, however, don't <i>have</i> to obsess. That's actually an important theme of the line; that you <i>choose</i> to damn yourself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T00:49:12-07:00","id":933305,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933302#post933302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mages, however, don't <i>have</i> to obsess.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your wife and children are going to be subject to a super-disease if you don't consume the secrets of Life from its spoor.<br /> <br /> Your home is going to draw down constant thunderstorms until you break the will of the thing that lives in the hospital incinerator and study the diagram that is its brain.<br /> <br /> Your place of work is going to become a font of ghosts if you don't do something about that suspicious coroner down at the police station.<br /> <br /> <b>I am not talking about mechanics.</b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T06:45:17-07:00","id":933396,"title":"","content":"Maybe if you are running at Gnosis 6. These things don't sound like &quot;subtle fractal geometry&quot;. And yeah, even mortals are going to do something if their GM throws that stuff at them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T08:55:55-07:00","id":933438,"title":"","content":"You can't really separate mechanics from setting; the entire <i>point</i> of mechanics is to simulate the setting. It gives a mechanical backbone to the story. If the mechanics don't support something, it won't usually happen, which was one of the big falling-down points of 1e Mage; mechanics not supporting intent.<br /> <br /> Also, what WHW said. I don't really consider it indicative of obsessive, inflexible behaviour if I have to solve problems being heaped on my doorstep. The Mage in those scenarios is <i>reacting</i> to things that are actively a hindrance to them. Obsession is an <i>active</i> pursuit; deliberately seeking things out of your own accord. I certainly wouldn't consider it an obsessive inflexibility if a mortal found out a vampire was feeding on their friends and neighbours and set to kill the bloodsucker; I'd consider it a bit of a lizard brain mentality but ultimately not an inappropriate reaction.<br /> <br /> Mages become monstrous by choice more often than necessity. It's not when the Mage needs to examine a super-disease to save their family that they're a monster; it's when they willingly inflict that disease on people to study it. It's not when they have to break the will of the thing living in the hospital incinerator, but do so because they're curious about its strange nature that so thoroughly purifies away illness and, thus, cause disease to become more common. It's not when their workplace is overwhelmed with ghosts, but when they deliberately overwhelm it so that &quot;accidents&quot; chase off people ahead of them for their promotion.<br /> <br /> Someone who doesn't care about something until it throws a blood-stained brick through their window aren't obsessed; they're reactive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T10:13:47-07:00","id":933468,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2855_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2855\" data-vbnamecard=\"2855\">Axelgear</a>, you made the argument that Mages are less monstrous than vampires and werewolves because Obsessions are all-carrot, no stick. Then when <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar111_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"111\" data-vbnamecard=\"111\">Satchel</a> pointed out that the long-term Nimbus is the stick, your rebuttal was that Mages are at their most monstrous when they pursue their Obsessions without being forced by external influence...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 07-18-2016, 01:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T10:50:27-07:00","id":933485,"title":"","content":"Nothing about the long-term Nimbus is remotely similar to what vamps and wolves have to endure. Even if, at its absolute worst, it surrounds a Mage with awfulness and horror, it's not the <i>Mage</i> that is horrific in this situation; it's the circumstances of life around them. Your long-term Nimbus doesn't force you to tear open people's throats for sustenance or fly into a blind rage if people are disrespectful towards you; it just creates circumstances around them.<br /> <br /> If someone cursed you that, wherever you went, bad luck and misfortune followed you, <i>you</i> wouldn't be a monster and nothing about your nature would be monstrous; it would be whomever cursed you and what they did that is monstrous. If you were a carrier for typhoid, nothing about <i>you</i> is monstrous. Your situation might be, but you aren't. You're just a human who had awful shit happen to you.<br /> <br /> Edit: And I want to add that a part of the reason why Mages can <i>be</i> monstrous is that their long-term Nimbus causing hooky shit to happen is, to most of them, a <i>boon</i> rather than a bane. Mages love having new junk to investigate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T11:32:52-07:00","id":933499,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I don't really think the long-term nimbus makes Mages monsters either. I just think &quot;Obsessions are all-carrot, no stick&quot; is a silly reason to cite for why Mages aren't monsters. Your arguments seem to me to support that stance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T11:37:04-07:00","id":933502,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I would love for the game to have some more sticks. Or at least tools to develop them, other than &quot;yeah, this should have consequences&quot;.<br /> If you want to make Obssession a stick, make it so that after X Time (and X time would decrease, based on Gnosis) of not pursuing your Obssession, you get a negative Condition. Then, after another interval, it upgrades to more severe version. And so on, until it eats you alive. Resolve these condition after induling in your Obssession.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T12:05:36-07:00","id":933514,"title":"","content":"I don't want that, WHW; it's never been necessary in any game I've run to have PCs be threatened into following their Obsessions. That's a part of the fun of the game and its themes; that it's not something Mages are forced to do, but something they dive into entirely of their own volition.<br /> <br /> Mages don't need to be forced to follow their Obsessions. They walk into Hell because they want to see what it looks like.<br /> <br /> @Charlaquin: Obsessions being all carrot, no stick, is why there's nothing intrinsically monstrous to the Awakened. A Mage can be an entirely upstanding member of the community, moderated and Wise, in ways a vamp or wolf never can. The vamp must feed, the wolf must hunt, but Mages don't have to obsess.<br /> <br /> We were talking about a specific contextual idea of monstrosity here, and Mages just aren't monstrous in the same way the other major splats are.<br /> <br /> When Mages are monstrous, it's in their own special, ultimately very human, way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T12:19:41-07:00","id":933522,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933438#post933438\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I certainly wouldn't consider it an obsessive inflexibility if a mortal found out a vampire was feeding on their friends and neighbours and set to kill the bloodsucker; I'd consider it a bit of a lizard brain mentality but ultimately not an inappropriate reaction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Once more with feeling:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Obsessive inflexibility of driving focus is, in fact, one of the defining qualities of most inhuman monsters in the setting. <b>Most gameline-level PCs get ringside tickets to what happens when you smush that trait up against a more rounded humanlike personality and wait for the former to overwhelm the latter.</b> </div> </div> </div>Your Obsessions are not your character and your character is not defined by their Obsessions, yes.<br /> <br /> Your Obsessions <i>are</i>, however, that part of your character's makeup that is most like the psychology-or-what-passes-for-it of monsters that have little care for self-control or the consequences of their actions, and they are the part that comes into the most regular prominence among mages as their Wisdom degenerates.<br /> <br /> <b>I am not saying that mages are inflexibly obsessive. I am saying that obsession is the inflexible element of a mage that brings them into alignment with monstrosity.</b><br /> <br /> Nitpicking about the intensity of my Long-Term Nimbus examples does nothing to change my point that a mage's Nimbus means they are an addict that attracts their drug of choice to them just as surely as a vampire or a werewolf living in a place occupied by other entities is going to have their respective urges to hunt for blood or secure their territory tested against their unwillingness to do those things. A low-Wisdom mage with a poorly-compartmentalized Shadow Name (if they even have one) is going to be running into little curiosities everywhere in the course of their mundane life, some of which will hurt people they care about simply by virtue of some of those people being Sleepers and some of those mysteries being Supernal or Abyssal.<br /> <br /> Babbling about typhoid just establishes that you've been paying zero attention to the definition of &quot;monster&quot; being used. You're welcome to disagree with it, but it is useless to claim that mages aren't monsters in a context where &quot;monster&quot; means, at bare minimum, &quot;occupies a framework of supernatural urges and involvement&quot; just because not all humans shy away from the supernatural and a mage who deliberately does nothing with their power over the things that are drawn to them could conceivably exist. Having typhoid does not inherently give you the ability to detect and control typhoid. A banshee is a monster, and all she does is make a noise when someone is about to die.<br /> <br /> If you refuse to accept that classification scheme, fine, but understand that if you proceed in the discussion without engaging with it then you're talking past the point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T12:34:59-07:00","id":933529,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933514#post933514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">@Charlaquin: Obsessions being all carrot, no stick, is why there's nothing intrinsically monstrous to the Awakened. A Mage can be an entirely upstanding member of the community, moderated and Wise, in ways a vamp or wolf never can.</div> </div> </div> </div>Can they really, though? I mean, sure, there's no mechanical negative reinforcement punishing them for not pursuing Obsessions, but you said yourself that's not something you would want anyway. The real question is, is avoiding Obsessions really something a Mage would do? I don't doubt some would try it. I do doubt many would be successful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933514#post933514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The vamp must feed, the wolf must hunt, but Mages don't have to obsess.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm gonna have to push back against that assertion. Mages don't have to <i>follow</i> their Obsessions, but they do have to <i>have</i> Obsessions. And while vampires have to feed, they don't have to feed on people. Even an Elder can survive on animal blood and/or cryogenically stored blood, etc. if they're committed enough. Werewolves have to hunt, but they can choose to only hunt animals, or Shartha, or spirits with negative or harmful resonance. It's a reoccurring theme in CofD games that just because you have to be a monster doesn't mean you have to be a bad person. Being a good person can be hard, especially for monsters. But it's a spectrum, not a dichotomy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933514#post933514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We were talking about a specific contextual idea of monstrosity here, and Mages just aren't monstrous in the same way the other major splats are.<br /> <br /> When Mages are monstrous, it's in their own special, ultimately very human, way.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would argue that all CofD monsters are monsters in very human ways.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T15:17:15-07:00","id":933602,"title":"","content":"I think that Mages tend towards obsession very strongly, but not all of them obsess in the same way that you'd usually categorise it. Like, the Interfector in the core book fiction. What's his obsession? It's possible that he has an obsession in a mystical sense, a tangible phenomena that he wishes to solve, but it seems to me that his obsession is to do a good job as that is all he has left. And is that really an obsession? Doesn't feel like it is, in the usual sense. It's what gets him out of bed in the morning, but it consumes him because there's nothing else there, perhaps. And Mage needs to let me play Outis, clearly.<br /> <br /> That's why I like that there's no negative reinforcement to not going after your Obsessions, other than a lack of personal development. Mage's are not instinctively driven like Vampires or Werewolves, neither should they be. If a Mage harms someone in the pursuit of their obsession, then they have no fall back of an inhuman drive to blame. That's all on them. But really, if a Werewolf goes into Kuruth and accidentally kills someone, it's not the same. They are responsible. But at the same time, no Werewolf can truly control their rage, and therefore they can be argued not fully be responsible for it (indeed, they can be argued to be victims in their own way of the Rage).<br /> <br /> Mages are monstrous to me in the same ways that humans are. That is, it's either a meaningless and entirely subjective statement or a poetically rich description of some people's actions depending on how you look at it. Mages have obsessions on their character sheet to reinforce the aesthetic and tone of the game, and if they don't follow them, they don't die or fall apart or whatever. But it is how Mages develop their power. And that's cool, because it gives an incentive to follow your character's obsessions. It's as easy for a Mage to be a good person as it is for the rest of us. They can benefit from not being a good person, just like the rest of us. And many Mages find it hard to rise above their desires, just like the rest of us. Sometimes those desires are admirable! Sometimes not.<br /> <br /> The difference between Humans and Mages is that the implications of their choice to indulge or deny are more far-reaching, due to their power. And that's scary, I suppose, in the sense of being a bystander to a Mage's obsession.<br /> <br /> But I think it's key to remember that a Mage doesn't have to be a monster. A Vampire or a Werewolf is, by it's nature, but not a Mage. Which is why the monstrosity of some Mages is all the more tragic and damning. They could have chosen a better way, but did not. I could hate a Mage for the effects of their blind obsession far more than I could hate a Werewolf for being unable to control it's rage. One is a condition, the other, a choice. And that's cool. Mages get the chance to be pretty bright heroes! Many of them are total bastards, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":117,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","user":"Solar","date":". 07-18-2016, 06:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-18T22:38:27-07:00","id":933737,"title":"","content":"Ok Heres the thing about mages. Humans can be very petty or passionate about the wildest of things in this big wide world of ours. The Difference between me and a mage is simple. Power. If something annoys me I have to deal with it. I can either do the socially accepted thing and asks for it to stop, or move. Or I chuck a brick at the offending thing. Mages have all the same passions and ideals, but they also have access to Lightening Bolts at a whim, Mind manipulators on call, and a Necormancer's Tome of &quot;Horrible things to do to your rivals when you're bored.&quot;<br /> <br /> Its the same conversation I have with people between the upper class and the lower class. They're both the same person, but one pays more for their mistakes, and one moods can touch farther horizons.<br /> <br /> Mages maybe awake and walking the wise path among the sleepers, But they're also the babysitter smoking, drinking and fucking their boyfriend while juggling fire torches around the house. So you can understand why witch hunters are so uppity","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Placeholder","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11731-mercset","name":"MercSet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11731_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"277"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T05:33:57-07:00","id":933809,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=932480#post932480\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A question for you guys is &quot;How human are mages in your chronicle?&quot; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>100% human, no question.about it They may be monsters, but that doesn't make them inhuman monsters. The Major explains it perfectly:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o8C9jJNif0A\" data-code=\"o8C9jJNif0A\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/o8C9jJNif0A/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> The reason why mages don't crumble when faced with too much trauma is not because of their inhumanity, but rather due to human's capacity to adapt. Humans in the CofD live in a world filled with monsters that constantly prey on them, so would it not make sense that they would bounce back? That they explore this horrifying world? That's what humans do! Mountains are there to climb. So humans adapt, Awakening to the Supernal to fulfill their potential in a world much harsher than ours, gaining power that far exceeds anything else, gram for gram (even demons and mummies, because creativity is the most potent weapon in any TRPG bar none).<br /> <br /> Does that make CofD a little Mage-centric? Yes, it does, and it should be so. Why? Because past all the fluff, vampires at their heart signify our greed and lust. Werewolves signify our primal savagery. Changelings signify our paranoia and madness. Prometheans signify our instinct to reject outsiders. Each timeless monster signifies an aspect(s) of our darker nature, that's the whole point of horror to begin with. So what do mages signify?<br /> <br /> They signify the best and worst aspect of humanity: potential. They are the dark lords that wield the same power as the hero does, forming classic villains like Darth Vader and Voldemort that stick in our hearts for the rest of our lives. Mages are us in every respect, making them the greatest of monsters. Vampires may have greed and lust, but so do mages. Werewolves may have primal savagery, but so do mages. There's simply nothing another monster can do that a mage can't, because mages have been formed from the very core of humanity itself.<br /> <br /> That's my take on it, anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2992-crapcarp","name":"crapcarp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T07:55:44-07:00","id":933882,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2992-crapcarp\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2992_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2992\" data-vbnamecard=\"2992\">crapcarp</a> <br /> <br /> I actually really like that scene. For a few reason and it maybe I've been playing too much star wars, but the ideal of will, the manipulative goating and the mechanical horror really has some Sith echos. When you think about it rationally, There is NO reason for hellsing to get that close. So the question is what is the major trying to accomplice with such a final gamble, other than having nothing to lose?<br /> <br /> I stipulate this. The villain very clearly believe in humanity's and man's will having power. The target of his power, his driving passion is conquest. And if nothing else he wishes to see that ideal proven right. Man&gt;Monsters argument. Now I do believe he was gambling, but he had a good bet. By playing on hellsing's question of is it human/right to use monsters? and this had been plaguing the hero for a time. The use of the monster had insured her survival from the time she was a child.<br /> <br /> Targeting like this, the question is also, does the hero deserve to survive or even live off the labor of the monster? So taking leave of her senses she now wishes to prove the major incorrect/wrong/ fuck you defiance. And by marching closer to the villain she wishes to demonstrate her power of will her defiance. And of course the villain is happy with this conclusion, either he'll get to take the hero down with him. Very much &quot;from hell's heart I stab at thy.&quot; Or He falls, but the he has proven the power of man utmost. A very Sith thing to do. Win/SpiteWin situation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Placeholder","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11731-mercset","name":"MercSet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11731_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"277"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T08:13:10-07:00","id":933898,"title":"","content":"A couple of nitpicks:<br /> <br /> 1. mages don't have to obsess in the same way that a character whose Vice is Lust doesn't have to lust. True, the mechanics are all-carrot/no-stick; but lustful character who <i>isn't</i> played as being lustful is being improperly roleplayed. <br /> 2. Technically, <i>Prometheans</i> don't signify our instinct to reject outsiders; the <i>torch-wielding mobs</i> do. What the Prometheans themselves signify is immaturity; poor impulse control. <br /> <br /> As for what negative characteristic mages signify? Hubris; pride, arrogance. A tendency to assume that the world revolves around you. Unrestrained Will. <br /> <br /> To me, <i>hunters</i> are the representatives of human potential; mages go <i>beyond</i> human potential, with every Attainment distancing a mage from the normal human experience. It's a game about transhumans and the dangers of transhumanism \u2014 and one of those dangers is a general lack of fear. <br /> <br /> The differences between Integrity and Wisdom are often subtle; but they're very real, and very profound. I generally wouldn't classify mages as <i>monstrous</i> (although they tend to have a disturbing sense of superiority that can easily lead to monstrous actions); but I'd definitely classify them among the <i>monsters</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 07-19-2016, 11:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T08:49:37-07:00","id":933920,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't say you're role-playing incorrectly if you don't indulge your vice/obsession. They are there to tempt, if you couldn't deny them that'd be pointless. No, a Mage can ignore their obsession. It's just hard and unusual. That's a perfectly fine character to play, even if you're not interested in it (I, personally, would be).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T08:55:44-07:00","id":933923,"title":"","content":"On the other hand if you choose a virtue or vice and aren't playing it out then perhaps you ought to be asking yourself what virtue and vice you're actually embodying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T09:49:04-07:00","id":933947,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933923#post933923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the other hand if you choose a virtue or vice and aren't playing it out then perhaps you ought to be asking yourself what virtue and vice you're actually embodying.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or alternatively you rise above your negative traits? People do that all the time, well, it's somewhat rare but it is a thing. I've played characters with a whole variety of Vices who just didn't indulge because they wanted to be better than that, and some even succeeded.<br /> <br /> The vice represents your character's darkest, most selfish trait and desire. Obsessions are kind of the same, and both work as a carrot that you can choose not to eat rather than a stick. And that's important.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T09:56:28-07:00","id":933951,"title":"","content":"Hey, Vices aren't &quot;dark and selfish and evil&quot; anymore. They switched to being your short-term means of comfort and regaining mental strength. A person who throws themselves to help others so they feel better might put &quot;Altruistic&quot; as their Vice. Someone really focused on getting on top, even if climbing bodies to do so, might put Ambitious or Ruthless as their Virtue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T10:40:51-07:00","id":933969,"title":"","content":"Right, my point isn't that you can't play a character trying to deny his vice, my point is that the way you play you may be playing a different vice than you thought you would be when you conceptualized the character. Saying your Vice is Lust and then never acting on that Vice implies that you're not playing a character who indulges in Lust, maybe his Vice is actually Sloth the way you're playing him (Just using the big 7 for ease of reference). If you give in to the Vice and act on it -then- you can say that's the character's Vice.<br /> <br /> This ties into the argument that you can play a Mage who doesn't obsess, it's either a false comparison or your Mage is obsessed with something you haven't realized they're actually obsessed with (Obsessed with not being Obsessed comes to mind).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T11:12:39-07:00","id":933984,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933898#post933898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To me, <i>hunters</i> are the representatives of human potential; mages go <i>beyond</i> human potential, with every Attainment distancing a mage from the normal human experience. It's a game about transhumans and the dangers of transhumanism \u2014 and one of those dangers is a general lack of fear.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very true, and probably part of why a lot of folks don't see them as monsters. Transhumanism is very popular.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T11:50:54-07:00","id":933997,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933969#post933969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Right, my point isn't that you can't play a character trying to deny his vice, my point is that the way you play you may be playing a different vice than you thought you would be when you conceptualized the character. Saying your Vice is Lust and then never acting on that Vice implies that you're not playing a character who indulges in Lust, maybe his Vice is actually Sloth the way you're playing him (Just using the big 7 for ease of reference). If you give in to the Vice and act on it -then- you can say that's the character's Vice.<br /> <br /> This ties into the argument that you can play a Mage who doesn't obsess, it's either a false comparison or your Mage is obsessed with something you haven't realized they're actually obsessed with (Obsessed with not being Obsessed comes to mind).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep; in which case, you should identify what your character's Obsession/Vice actually is and petition the Storyteller to adjust your character sheet so that you can start getting the appropriate rewards. Because as is, you're rejecting the carrot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T12:01:36-07:00","id":934001,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=933969#post933969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Right, my point isn't that you can't play a character trying to deny his vice, my point is that the way you play you may be playing a different vice than you thought you would be when you conceptualized the character. Saying your Vice is Lust and then never acting on that Vice implies that you're not playing a character who indulges in Lust, maybe his Vice is actually Sloth the way you're playing him (Just using the big 7 for ease of reference). If you give in to the Vice and act on it -then- you can say that's the character's Vice.<br /> <br /> This ties into the argument that you can play a Mage who doesn't obsess, it's either a false comparison or your Mage is obsessed with something you haven't realized they're actually obsessed with (Obsessed with not being Obsessed comes to mind).</div> </div> </div> </div>...or someone whose vice is lust but who doesn't indulge that desire? Like, you're suggesting that this means the character is indulging their real deeper desire, but that's not always or even often going to be the case. It's a bit shit to assume a character who isn't indulging their vice is just being roleplayed wrong, IMO.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T12:06:35-07:00","id":934005,"title":"","content":"It's fine if you're not indulging it and showing the struggle, but how many times do you deny it and it remains plausible that it's what you actually struggle with?<br /> <br /> I'm not saying the player is roleplaying wrong, I don't care how they roleplay. I'm just raising the question of whether or not they're actually getting everything out of the mechanics that they can if they keep ignoring them.<br /> <br /> Vice is a carrot mechanic, you're rewarded for indulging it. Denying it is roleplaying masochism. That's cool if it's what you're into.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 07-19-2016, 03:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T12:10:31-07:00","id":934010,"title":"","content":"I'd say ignoring your Vice is a valid roleplaying choice, but a strange one since you're intentionally depriving yourself of potential Willpower. Likewise ignoring Obsessions and depriving yourself of Arcane Beats.<br /> <br /> Personally, I prefer to make roleplaying choices that contribute to an effective character rather than ones that detract from it. But to each their own.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T12:27:22-07:00","id":934017,"title":"","content":"Or let's put it another way, Mages being obsessive is part of the buy in of being in a Mage game. It's like saying you're going Vegan but you'll still eat eggs. Well, I'm sorry but you're not Vegan then. You may be very similar and still achieve a great deal of personal satisfaction and there's certainly nothing wrong with it, but that doesn't make you vegan. I'm not going to tell you you're living life wrong, I'm just going to point out you're using the wrong label on yourself, as well as laboring under wrong assumptions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 07-19-2016, 03:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T13:23:56-07:00","id":934052,"title":"","content":"I don't think it's strange at all for a character to deny their desires if they think their desires are morally wrong, which lots of people do. I agree that it's interesting for a character to struggle with that, but I think it's important that you don't have to and indeed that it's possible to deny your base urges. It makes the indulgence more significant if and when it does happen! The genre does call for it but it's not mandatory and besides if the character works and is fun to play for everyone who cares if it's not necessarily genre appropriate.<br /> <br /> The whole vegan analogy is just, like, look I don't really think it works and lets leave it at that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T13:33:57-07:00","id":934057,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934052#post934052\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think it's strange at all for a character to deny their desires if they think their desires are morally wrong, which lots of people do.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're comparing ignoring one of the things that's supposed to tempt that character in particular with denial. If a pillar of your personality never comes up it's not much of a character element. Apathy is not resistance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T13:42:26-07:00","id":934062,"title":"","content":"You seem to be completed missing the point <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar117_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"117\" data-vbnamecard=\"117\">Solar</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T13:59:02-07:00","id":934070,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934062#post934062\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You seem to be completed missing the point <span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span></div> </div> </div> </div>No I understand your point, I just don't agree with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T14:15:27-07:00","id":934081,"title":"","content":"Thinking about it we may be arguing on a different scale of inaction. You claim that denying a Vice or an Obsession can be character defining. I've already granted that on a micro-scale that this may be true, but that's not what I'm addressing. On the macro, if you claim a Mage is capable of simply continuing to deny his Obsession or a character his Vice then you are essentially playing without those character facets and are no longer playing the actual game. Your game might look similar, but it is in fact different. I'm addressing the macro implications because someone said that Mages can just ignore their Obsession and thus aren't monsters. From what you seem to be saying, by my reading, you're insistent on pursuing an argument focused on the micro-instance of denying a Vice or Obsession, but since that is in fact not what I'm talking about you're missing the point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T15:10:57-07:00","id":934123,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934052#post934052\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think it's strange at all for a character to deny their desires if they think their desires are morally wrong, which lots of people do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Strange from a meta-game perspective. You're making the choice to deprive yourself of Willpower or Arcane Beats for the sake of &quot;doing what my character would do&quot; when you could just as easily choose to play a character who &quot;would do&quot; the thing that gets you Willpower/Arcane Beats.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934052#post934052\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree that it's interesting for a character to struggle with that, but I think it's important that you don't have to and indeed that it's possible to deny your base urges. It makes the indulgence more significant if and when it does happen! The genre does call for it but it's not mandatory and besides if the character works and is fun to play for everyone who cares if it's not necessarily genre appropriate.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like I said, valid roleplaying choice... Just... Why?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T15:31:04-07:00","id":934134,"title":"","content":"Saying it's important that you can play someone who denies their base urges in this case is akin to saying it's important that you can play a game of Dungeons and Dragons without ever doing a combat encounter-sure, it's feasibly possible, but given how much blood and ink is spilt over the combat engine, it's obvious someone is not using the game as intended, to their detriment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T15:43:47-07:00","id":934143,"title":"","content":"Yeah but at the end of the day what my character would do is up to me. What my character is obsessed with is written on the sheet, sure, but that doesn't mean I have to engage with the obsession if I feel that it suits how I want to play. Let's say my Mage has the chance to go and take care of a friend in need, or indulge in their obsession. I might choose to have them take care of their friend in need. Sure, there's a cost. That obsession gives Arcane Experience! But if I feel that my character would deny that obsession to go and take care of that friend, to try and be better than their own basic desire to explore the mysteries, then that's fine too. It's the denial of the carrot that makes the sacrifice an interesting roleplaying action; sometimes to be a good person you must turn away from something that gives a reward and indeed the game mechanises this by not giving me the Arcane Beat I would get for going after that mystery.<br /> <br /> Just because I write it on my character sheet doesn't mean that it's what my character needs to do. My character's form exists in my head, and the sheet is merely trying to express that to the best of the mechanic's ability (how suitably Platonic for Mage!) I like getting XP (my friends joke that I am a total XP whore haha) but at the same time, I like playing my characters as I feel they should be. Why do it? Because I find it more satisfying to sometimes not indulge and obsession or a vice than to indulge it, indeed for some characters, deliberately denying their desires when they clash with other priorities can be something they choose to do on purpose. And that doesn't mean that those desires are actually a cloaked need for denial. I had anger management problems, I spent a long time getting over them. I deliberately try to avoid losing my temper and indulging that desire, but that doesn't mean that I secretly deep down don't really want to be angry, I want to be disciplined. No, I do want to be angry. I just choose to try and not to be.<br /> <br /> Anyway;<br /> <br /> Mages can ignore their Obsession and avoid committing monstrous acts in the name of their obsessions in Mage the Awakening 2nd edition as it is written right now. That's playing the game, black and white (more a bluish-grey, really) in the rulebook. I can indulge my obsession, and I can choose to say my character really likes to investigate this mystery but won't do so for whatever reason. There's no mechanics to force you to do that. There's plenty of rewards for investigating those mysteries, but no way to force the character to do it or inflict negative consequences simply as a result of no doing so. You can say &quot;you aren't playing the game!&quot; all you like, but I am. The rules. As written.<br /> <br /> Now, I feel like actually the vast majority of characters will never go down this route. But I think it's important that they can. Because at the end of the day it means a Mage is <i>responsible</i> for indulging their obsessions. There's no excuse of &quot;oh well if you don't you get forced to do so by X Y Z&quot; like how a Werewolf can just be in a situation where they go into Kuruth because you didn't roll enough successes on your pool to resist. And that's cool, that's part of Werewolf, but equally part of Mage is how you could choose to not investigate this mystery. The only person forcing you to do it is you. And therefore when people get hurt as a by-line of a Mage's obsession, that's on them. That's their deal. You cannot blame a thing you are not responsible for, the Great Curse, Kuruth, whatever. You <i>choose</i> to indulge Obsessions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T16:13:16-07:00","id":934154,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934143#post934143\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah but at the end of the day what my character would do is up to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly, so why not choose to play a character who would do the things that the game rewards you for doing?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934143#post934143\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What my character is obsessed with is written on the sheet, sure, but that doesn't mean I have to engage with the obsession if I feel that it suits how I want to play.</div> </div> </div> </div>Granted, but why not choose Obsessions that do suit how you want to play?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934143#post934143\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let's say my Mage has the chance to go and take care of a friend in need, or indulge in their obsession. I might choose to have them take care of their friend in need. Sure, there's a cost. That obsession gives Arcane Experience! But if I feel that my character would deny that obsession to go and take care of that friend, to try and be better than their own basic desire to explore the mysteries, then that's fine too. It's the denial of the carrot that makes the sacrifice an interesting roleplaying action; sometimes to be a good person you must turn away from something that gives a reward and indeed the game mechanises this by not giving me the Arcane Beat I would get for going after that mystery.</div> </div> </div> </div>Totally valid roleplaying choice, but also a highly context-dependent one. Eventually, you're probably going to run into a situation where following the Obsession is the more appealing choice to your character. If there's not, you might be doing what is sometimes referred to in D&amp;D parlance as &quot;playing a defective adventurer.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934143#post934143\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just because I write it on my character sheet doesn't mean that it's what my character needs to do. My character's form exists in my head, and the sheet is merely trying to express that to the best of the mechanic's ability (how suitably Platonic for Mage!) I like getting XP (my friends joke that I am a total XP whore haha) but at the same time, I like playing my characters as I feel they should be. Why do it? Because I find it more satisfying to sometimes not indulge and obsession or a vice than to indulge it, indeed for some characters, deliberately denying their desires when they clash with other priorities can be something they choose to do on purpose. And that doesn't mean that those desires are actually a cloaked need for denial. I had anger management problems, I spent a long time getting over them. I deliberately try to avoid losing my temper and indulging that desire, but that doesn't mean that I secretly deep down don't really want to be angry, I want to be disciplined. No, I do want to be angry. I just choose to try and not to be.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's all well and good, but there's a difference between sometimes choosing to not indulge an Obsession or a Vice and never indulging in it. If you want to roleplay a character who struggles with anger-management issues, that character should <i>struggle</i> with it. If they never give into their anger, they've successfully managed it. Great job, take a Beat for fulfilling that long-term Aspiration and pick a new Vice cause this one isn't relevant to the story any more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T16:20:32-07:00","id":934158,"title":"","content":"Nm, beating a dead horse.<br /> <br /> On a different note, since Mages get to choose what to be Obsessed with then ultimately it's still their fault for whatever actions they take, becoming Obsessed is an action. So no, there isn't a need to deny Obsession in order to experience the horror of mismanaged responsibility.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 07-19-2016, 07:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T16:23:59-07:00","id":934159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934154#post934154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exactly, so why not choose to play a character who would do the things that the game rewards you for doing?<br /> <br /> <br /> Granted, but why not choose Obsessions that do suit how you want to play?<br /> <br /> <br /> Totally valid roleplaying choice, but also a highly context-dependent one. Eventually, you're probably going to run into a situation where following the Obsession is the more appealing choice to your character. If there's not, you might be doing what is sometimes referred to in D&amp;D parlance as &quot;playing a defective adventurer.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> That's all well and good, but there's a difference between sometimes choosing to not indulge an Obsession or a Vice and never indulging in it. If you want to roleplay a character who struggles with anger-management issues, that character should <i>struggle</i> with it. If they never give into their anger, they've successfully managed it. Great job, take a Beat for fulfilling that long-term Aspiration and pick a new Vice cause this one isn't relevant to the story any more.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am pretty sure you can't pick obsessions which say you put your friends and family before your mystic investigations. They have to be sorcerous obsessions<br /> <br /> EDIT: But anyway, the point is the denial of indulgence and sacrifice of something because you believe in taking a better path. Being a better person, over being a &quot;better&quot; Mage. Giving up the Arcane Beat in the pursuit of a more abstract moral righteousness. So yeah, the mechanics reflect that. And probably your Mage characters won't a lot of the time (mine neither) or need to make that choice because it doesn't always come up, point is, there's an entirely legit way to play the game where you choose not to follow your mystical obsessions in pursuit of a more abstract desire to be a good person because you feel they clash, and that's an interesting Mage character to play. I'd deliberately choose obsessions which sacrifice morality in order to deliberately ignore those obsessions to be a better person and roleplay the struggle that might cause a Mage (note; struggle does not mean ever giving in, it can mean that you find it hard and roleplay that but stick to your guns. I'm weirdly always reminded of, like, Aragorn here. He struggles with his desire to take the Ring. Never does, though) because that's a cool character, tbh.<br /> <br /> I should say, I think the system right now does a perfect job of doing exactly what I want it to do. Which is cool!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":117,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","user":"Solar","date":". 07-19-2016, 07:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T16:24:46-07:00","id":934160,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934158#post934158\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nm, beating a dead horse.</div> </div> </div> </div>If it's any consolation, I am as absolutely sure that you're wrong as I bet you're sure I am, so don't worry about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T16:34:00-07:00","id":934165,"title":"","content":"There's a balance to &quot;It's my character, I do what I want&quot; and &quot;Why are you playing this game?&quot;<br /> <br /> This game is about non-aspirational people who constantly have the supernatural ping off in their waking world and cannot, in the end, do anything but run into the mysteries around the corners. It is not merely a game about the abuse of power, but the obsessions and drives that justify that abuse, the things that make it &quot;all right&quot; in the end.<br /> <br /> Play around with it, dance around it it, pull towards it and push away to it, but that's the bar this game is built around. If, in the end, you can always set your all important question down, you might need a different game. Mages don't settle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T16:39:52-07:00","id":934170,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934165#post934165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's a balance to &quot;It's my character, I do what I want&quot; and &quot;Why are you playing this game?&quot;<br /> <br /> This game is about non-aspirational people who constantly have the supernatural ping off in their waking world and <b>cannot, in the end, do anything but run into the mysteries around the corners.</b> It is not merely a game about the abuse of power, but the obsessions and drives that justify that abuse, the things that make it &quot;all right&quot; in the end.<br /> <br /> Play around with it, dance around it it, pull towards it and push away to it, but that's the bar this game is built around. If, in the end, you can always set your all important question down, you might need a different game. Mages don't settle.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Sure, you tell yourself that if it makes you feel better.&quot;<br /> <br /> That's half a joke, but I think it's relevant. They can. They can choose not to do it. That's kind of important when it comes to dealing with the fallout of them not doing it.<br /> <br /> Also bonus points for the forum classic &quot;this might not be the game for you&quot; line (if I had a penny for every time I heard that...). I bought Mage of my own volition! I played it a bunch in first ed and I'm going to run a campaign I'm planning and hopefully play it too! I'm pretty sure I know it's for me, at this point. I just think it's integral to the theme of Mage that they do not, at the end of the day, have to do that. They don't. They like to tell themselves they do, but they don't. When their personal lives fall apart and people get hurt and X Y Z happens, Mages like to say, but I have to know the answer! Sure, that's true. You do care more about these mysteries than you do your families and friends! Nobody made you make those sacrifices. And that's cool. Most of my characters would end up like that, and lie to themselves about it. But that's why it's really important there's a carrot and not a stick to your obsessions as a Mage. Because then it's tempting and all. On. <i>You.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T16:47:22-07:00","id":934175,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/932459-wisdom-vs-integrity?p=934170#post934170\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's half a joke, but I think it's relevant. They can. They can choose not to do it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Long-Term Nimbus. You made your choice and the fallout of it lasts for the rest of your life. You can try to close your eyes and cover your ears to it, but becoming a mage means becoming a person is drawn to mysteries. Marple's namesake isn't the only detective that seems to attract trouble.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also bonus points for the forum classic &quot;this might not be the game for you&quot; line (<b>if I had a penny for every time I heard that...</b>). </div> </div> </div>You would have two cents.<br /> <br /> And wouldn't you know it, there it is.<br /> <br /> Spent.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T16:55:14-07:00","id":934180,"title":"","content":"I'd probably have, like<br /> <br /> twenty pence actually<br /> <br /> Which is just enough for a Freddo these days!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T17:06:59-07:00","id":934189,"title":"","content":"You know, you could just pick an Obsession &quot;become Wiser&quot; and call it a day. Or &quot;Find ways to make my family safe from magical threats&quot;. I feel that you consider indulging in Vice and Obsession some sort of moral failure. Again, if your character has anger problems, why pick Anger as a Vice? It's more system-hygenic to instead take a Persistent &quot;Anger Management&quot; Condition that rewards you Beats for heroically spending Willpower points to not get Angry, and put &quot;Family Person&quot; or &quot;Self-Sacrificing&quot; or &quot;Workaholic&quot; in your Vice slot, so your Vice slot is something you actually do.<br /> <br /> A dude who regularly walks between fighting people to stop them from beating each other up and ending up being punched in a face can be fulfilling his Vice by doing this, if it's what makes him sleep at night.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custo